// ============================================================================
== 1.1 The Ten Marks and Where They Sit

The Use of English paper has sixty questions and four blocks. The reading text — the
prescribed novel — is the third block of Section A, and it carries ten questions. That is
one sixth of the paper, and it is the only sixth with a closed and short syllabus: twelve
chapters, about sixty pages, one author, one cast.

#concept-box("The shape of the paper, and the shape of the text")[
  *Section A* carries twenty-five questions: a comprehension passage (five), a cloze passage (ten) and the reading text (ten).

  *Section B* carries twenty-five: sentence interpretation, antonyms, synonyms and sentence completion.

  *Section C* carries ten oral-form questions.

  The reading text is therefore the single largest block in the paper that can be prepared to the last question. A candidate cannot know what passage will be set; every candidate can know what happens in Chapter 7.
]

The questions are not literary criticism. They are comprehension questions with a book
behind them: who did what, in which chapter, in what order, and what it shows about the
person who did it. The examiners are not asking whether you enjoyed the novel. They are
asking whether you read it.

#rule-box("The five things the ten questions test")[
  + *Fact.* A name, a place, a sum, a title — one clause in one chapter.
  + *Sequence.* Which of two events came first, or what happened immediately before or after another.
  + *Character.* What a person is like, proved by something the person did.
  + *Theme and technique.* What an episode or a title or a symbol is doing in the book.
  + *Detail under pressure.* A minor character, a small object, a repeated phrase — the questions that separate a reader from a skimmer.
]

#trap-box("The candidate who has “read the summary”")[
  A summary on a study site gives you the plot and none of the details, and the details are where the marks are. The novel has roughly forty named characters; a two-page summary names eight. A candidate who has read only the summary can answer the theme questions and loses the fact questions, which are the majority.
]

// ============================================================================
== 1.2 The Four Shapes

Every reading-text question is one of four shapes, and the shape tells you where the
answer lives. Name the shape before you read the options, and half the work is done
before you have chosen anything.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_four_shapes.svg",
  "The four question shapes, the words that announce each one, and where in your reading the answer sits.", width: 100%)

#shortcut-box("The shape, read off the stem in three seconds")[
  + A *who*, *what*, *where* or *how much* in the stem: a fact question. Go to the card.
  + *First*, *before*, *after*, *immediately*, *arrange*: a sequence question. Go to the timeline.
  + An adjective in the options — *patient*, *stubborn*, *generous*: a character question. Go to the act.
  + *Illustrates*, *suggests*, *shows*, *the title*, *the narrator*: a theme or technique question. Go to the pattern.
]

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The fact question nobody misses",
  [The novel is set mainly in

    A. Abuja \
    B. Lekki, Lagos \
    C. Ibadan \
    D. London],
  [
    *Step 1 — name the shape.* *Where* in the stem, four place names in the options: a fact question, and the easiest one on the paper.

    *Step 2 — the card.* Stardom Schools is in Lekki, in Lagos State. The novel's title says so before the first page does.

    *Step 3 — kill the alternatives.* Abuja and Ibadan are Nigerian cities the novel mentions or uses but does not set the school in; London is where Seri and the children live and where Bepo is going — a real place in the book, which is exactly why it is offered.

    *Check.* Say the four settings of the novel out loud: Lekki and Stardom, Beesway, Badagry, and the United Kingdom. Only one of them holds the school. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The nickname in the title",
  [The title of the novel refers to

    A. a street in Lekki \
    B. the founder of Stardom Schools \
    C. the nickname Bepo earned at the school \
    D. a newspaper column about education],
  [
    *Step 1 — name the shape.* The question asks what the title refers to: a theme-and-technique question wearing a fact question's clothes.

    *Step 2 — the text.* Bepo is known at Stardom as *The Lekki Headmaster* — a nickname he earned over twenty-four years, and one that plays on the title of the old television drama *The Village Headmaster*.

    *Step 3 — the traps.* B is the founder, a real figure in the school's history but not the title; A and D are inventions of the examiner, plausible because the novel is full of Lagos street names and school talk.

    *Check.* A title question is answered by the person or idea the whole book is about. The book is about Bepo, and the nickname is his. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "The chapter the assembly belongs to",
  [In which chapter does Bepo first tell the staff that he is leaving the country?

    A. Chapter 1 \
    B. Chapter 2 \
    C. Chapter 6 \
    D. Chapter 12],
  [
    *Step 1 — name the shape.* *Which chapter* — a fact question with a sequence edge.

    *Step 2 — the timeline.* Chapter 1 is the breakdown: he cannot speak, and the MD sends for Pastor Ope Wande. The admission comes five days later, in Chapter 2, *The Enticement*.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* A is the obvious trap, because the assembly is the famous scene and candidates remember it as the announcement. C is the grammar row and D is the return — both real, both irrelevant.

    *Check.* If the question says *first*, ask what the character knew at that point. In Chapter 1 nobody knows, including Bepo. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "The stem that tells you its own shape",
  [“Which of the following happened immediately after the morning assembly?” This question is best answered by

    A. recalling the chapter that contains the assembly \
    B. placing the events of the scene on a timeline \
    C. deciding which character is most important \
    D. working out the theme of the chapter],
  [
    *Step 1 — the word.* *Immediately after* is the tell. The question is a sequence question, whatever its subject.

    *Step 2 — the move.* A sequence question is settled by order, and order is what a timeline holds. Recalling a chapter (A) tells you where to look but not what comes next.

    *Step 3 — the other options.* C and D answer character and theme questions, not this one. They are true statements about good reading and wrong answers to this question — the *true irrelevance* in its purest form.

    *Check.* Match the move to the word in the stem. *Immediately* wants order. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "The negative question, and the plausible invention",
  [Which of the following is NOT stated in the novel?

    A. Bepo's salary at Stardom is ₦400,000 a month. \
    B. Seri works in the United Kingdom as a nurse. \
    C. Bepo took a degree in education at the University of Lagos. \
    D. Bepo's post in the United Kingdom pays £3,600 a month.],
  [
    *Step 1 — mark the negative.* NOT is the most important word in the stem, and it is set in the same size as every other word.

    *Step 2 — check the three you know.* A is the salary the novel gives him; B is Seri's work; D is the figure his colleagues quote for the job waiting for him. All three are in the text.

    *Step 3 — the invention.* C sounds entirely reasonable — a headmaster with a degree — and the novel does not say it. A plausible detail that the text never supplies is the commonest *NOT* answer.

    *Check.* The wrong answers here are true and the right answer is merely absent. Underline NOT before you read A. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

// ============================================================================
== 1.3 The Twelve Cards

The novel is short enough to read three times and too long to remember by accident.
What makes it stay is a file: twelve cards, one per chapter, each with the same four
columns. The cards take about ninety minutes to make and they replace the third reading.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_fact_file.svg",
  "The four columns of a chapter card. The first two are for fact questions; the last two are for character and theme questions.", width: 100%)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_two_reads.svg",
  "Two reads and two revisions. The second read is the one that earns the marks, because it is the only one done with a pen.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("What a card looks like when it is finished")[
  *Chapter 5 — Snake in the Roof.* *What happens:* the MD finds staff cars parked on school land, suspects the finances, and the board discovers a cooperative purse of ₦95 million; loans are capped at ₦250,000. *Who:* Mrs Ibidun Gloss, Chief Mrs Solape Bayo, the security guard who reports the cars, Mr Jeremi Amos. *What it shows:* the school's money is real, and the board fears the staff more than it trusts them. *The line:* *it is like hanging a snake in the roof and going to bed.*

  Four lines. Every fact question on Chapter 5 is answerable from them, and the last column answers the theme question the examiner builds from the chapter's title.
]

#rule-box("How to write a card that works")[
  + Write it in your own words. A copied sentence is not a memory; it is a transcript.
  + Keep the first column to one line. If it needs three, you have not decided what the chapter is for.
  + Put every named person in column two, including the ones who only speak once.
  + Choose the quotation because it is memorable, not because it is beautiful. You need to find it again under pressure.
]

// ============================================================================
== 1.4 The Five Ways a Wrong Option Is Built

Three of the four options in a reading-text question are wrong, and they are wrong in
predictable ways. A candidate who can name the lie can often discard two options before
deciding anything.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_lie_shapes.svg",
  "The five shapes of a wrong option, what each one looks like on the screen, and the check that kills it.", width: 100%)

#trap-box("The half-truth is the expensive one")[
  *“Bepo refuses to join the ritual at Beesway, and is sacked for it.”* The first clause is true; the second is not — he is struck on the head and leaves. An option that begins correctly is read quickly, and the eye stops at the comma. Read to the full stop, every time.
]

#shortcut-box("Three eliminations in ten seconds")[
  + *Find the absolute.* *Always*, *never*, *only*, *entirely* — a novel about compromise rarely supports one.
  + *Find the echo.* An option that repeats a word from the passage and adds no idea is a plant.
  + *Find the wrong name.* A real character attached to the wrong deed is the commonest lie in a fact question.
]

// ============================================================================
== 1.5 The Reading Plan and the Exam-Hall Procedure

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_read_plan.svg",
  "Four weeks, about half an hour a day, and the whole novel revised in twenty minutes by the end of it.", width: 100%)

In the hall the ten questions take about four minutes, and they should be taken first.
They are the only questions in the paper whose answers are entirely inside your own head;
the comprehension passage still has to be read. Bank the ten, then spend the time you have
saved on the passage that pays for it.

#rule-box("The exam-hall procedure for the reading text")[
  + *Read all ten stems before answering any.* The names and chapters in later stems often remind you of the answer to an earlier one.
  + *Answer the fact questions in one pass.* If the card does not produce the answer in twenty seconds, flag it and move.
  + *Do the theme and character questions second.* They take longer and they are worth the same mark.
  + *Return to the flags with eight minutes left.* A flagged fact question is usually solved by a name you met in a later stem.
  + *Never leave a blank.* A quarter of a guess is a mark, and the paper has no penalty.
]

#diagram-fig("diagrams/recap_ch01.svg",
  "Chapter 1 recap card: the reading text in the exam.", width: 74%)

#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-mint, stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-green, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 1 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    The reading text is ten of the sixty questions, the largest block in the paper with a closed syllabus, and it is answered from a file rather than from memory. Four shapes cover it: fact, sequence, character, and theme or technique — and the shape is visible in the stem before you read a single option. Twelve cards, one per chapter, with four columns each: what happens, who is there, what it shows, and one line worth keeping. Two reads, the second with the cards open; a third revision of the cards alone takes twenty minutes. Three of the four options are wrong in one of five patterned ways, and three of those five can be eliminated without knowing the answer. In the hall, take the ten first, answer the facts in one pass, flag what will not come, and leave nothing blank.
  ]
]

== 1.6 Chapter 1 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [The reading text carries how many questions in the Use of English paper?], [ten], [five], [fifteen], [twenty])

#mcq(2, [Which of the following is NOT one of the four question shapes used for the reading text?], [fact], [sequence], [etymology], [character])

#mcq(3, [Bepo is the principal of], [Beesway Group of Schools], [Stardom Schools], [Fruitful Future School], [Stardom Kiddies])

#mcq(4, [An option that is true of the novel but does not answer the question asked is called], [the wrong name], [the half-truth], [the true irrelevance], [the overstatement])

#answer-key-grid(("A", "C", "B", "C"))

#answer-notes((
  ("A", [Ten: one sixth of the sixty-question paper. B is the comprehension passage, C is the cloze plus the comprehension, and D is an invention.]),
  ("C", [Etymology — the history of a word — is not a shape the section uses. The fourth shape is theme and technique, which A, B and D each name a member of.]),
  ("B", [Stardom Schools, Lekki. A and C are the two schools in his past, and D is the nursery and primary arm of Stardom, where he was headmaster before he was principal — the best trap in the set.]),
  ("C", [The true irrelevance. A is a real character wrongly attached, B is a half-true sentence, and D is an absolute the text cannot support.]),
))

